Kevin Callen lives in Peterborough, Ontario, in one of my favorite parts of the world

90
00:04:26,420 --> 00:04:32,700
where we have gone for the past many years to go up and do a canoe trip with our venture crew.

91
00:04:33,220 --> 00:04:34,620
That's part of our troop

92
00:04:34,620 --> 00:04:38,500
And I've read a lot of Kevin's material

93
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:43,960
And we've seen his videos- Happy Camper videos- on the podcast

94
00:04:44,720 --> 00:04:48,300
And he was gracious enough to sit down with me and do an interview here recently.

95
00:04:48,780 --> 00:04:51,220
There will be some links and things mentioned in the interview

96
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:58,600
And those will be on the post that contains this podcast at scoutmastertypepadcom.

97
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:04,220
And if you're truly interested in Ontario and canoeing- I've been up there a number of times

98
00:05:04,360 --> 00:05:06,760
And I can point you to some good resources-

99
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:14,740
And just email me, ClarkGreen C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-N at gmailcom

100
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:20,460
And I can help you sort out what goes on up there and how to put a trip together for your troop.

101
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:24,020
So that's an awful lot to get done in one podcast

102
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:26,340
And I think we should get started, shall we?

103
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:50,940
Today I'm talking with Canadian author and canoe enthusiast, Kevin Callen, who is also known as the Happy Camper,

104
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:55,940
And we've seen some of his videos on the Scoutmaster blog before.

105
00:05:56,420 --> 00:05:57,180
How are you doing, Kevin?

106
00:05:57,620 --> 00:05:58,300
How are you doing Clark?

107
00:05:58,300 --> 00:05:59,100
I am happy,

108
00:05:59,400 --> 00:05:59,940
Are you happy?

109
00:06:00,380 --> 00:06:03,400
I am ecstatic because I'm talking to you

110
00:06:03,560 --> 00:06:12,840
And your book about the Algonquin Provincial Park and canoe routes in Algonquin was really instrumental into us building a program where we bring our scouts up that way.

111
00:06:13,160 --> 00:06:13,720
Well, that's great.

112
00:06:13,840 --> 00:06:15,120
What do you think of Algonquin?

113
00:06:15,440 --> 00:06:17,320
It's an amazingly different place.

114
00:06:17,640 --> 00:06:24,300
I'm convinced you could go paddling in Algonquin and around there for 20 years and never see the same water twice.

115
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:24,960
Oh, definitely,

116
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:26,120
Yeah, there's lots of places to go.

117
00:06:26,180 --> 00:06:27,200
That's the nice thing about the park,

118
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:29,500
I mean, I only live an hour away from it,

119
00:06:29,580 --> 00:06:32,039
So that's the nicest thing too, is I can just head up and paddle.

120
00:06:32,479 --> 00:06:36,400
My favorite place is the Nipissing River on the northwest corner

121
00:06:37,039 --> 00:06:42,039
And it's just one of those tranquil little trout rivers that's hard to get to.

122
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:46,300
It's got old-growth forest with old-growth pine hanging.

123
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:48,380
It's just a beautiful place

124
00:06:48,620 --> 00:06:50,400
Because it's like a transition zone.

125
00:06:50,500 --> 00:07:02,800
The neat thing about Algonquin is that the forest ecology that's around that area continues south through southern Ontario or southeastern Ontario and then right into the main area of the United States,

126
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:07,520
And it's this sort of corridor that's a lot of wildlife travel

127
00:07:07,640 --> 00:07:09,040
They've figured out the last few years.

128
00:07:09,140 --> 00:07:12,740
They've tracked wolves and everything else and moose that travel this corridor all the time

129
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:15,340
And it's kind of neat that we actually have it.

130
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:16,800
And now let me get this right:

131
00:07:17,220 --> 00:07:18,920
Is it portaging or portaging

132
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:20,900
Well, it depends on where you live.

133
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:24,600
It doesn't matter how you say it,

134
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:25,760
Everybody hates one.

135
00:07:26,220 --> 00:07:32,660
Bill Macy said it best when he said people actually like portaging is either a liar or crazy.

136
00:07:33,780 --> 00:07:40,680
I've got to say, though, the only thing that I think is left, though, that keeps wilderness wild, is a long portage,

137
00:07:40,680 --> 00:07:50,540
Because, you think about it, if you go three miles with a canoe on your head and a pack on your shoulder and stuff, what you're going to get at the end is going to be worth it.

138
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:51,300
Oh yeah,

139
00:07:51,380 --> 00:07:54,280
I don't care how people get into the woods. to be quite honest,

140
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:55,240
I'm not a purist,

141
00:07:56,060 --> 00:07:59,640
But I know a lot of people that drive the ATVs and stuff,

142
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:00,560
And I'll go up to them.

143
00:08:00,620 --> 00:08:06,040
I said, look, you always get on my case that you go into this lake, and it's not what it was a few years back.

144
00:08:06,340 --> 00:08:08,880
The reason why is it's too easy for you to get into here now,

145
00:08:08,880 --> 00:08:14,040
And as soon as it's too easy to get in, then the wilderness concept is gone and the trout are gone,

146
00:08:14,280 --> 00:08:20,140
And if you actually put a canoe on your back and portage for three miles, then what you're going to get is going to be worth it,

147
00:08:20,260 --> 00:08:22,659
And I think that they're not getting, that

148
00:08:22,820 --> 00:08:24,640
They all want everything easy for them.

149
00:08:25,240 --> 00:08:32,620
And the nice thing is if you meet someone at the end of the portage, a really long portage- if you actually do see someone, they're going to be just like you

150
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:35,140
Because they actually went through the same thing that you just did.

151
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:46,120
Well, and we should explain for the non-canoe heads out there: a portage or a portage where you take all your gear, but basically you're backpacking with a canoe.

152
00:08:46,620 --> 00:08:46,860
Yeah,

153
00:08:47,640 --> 00:08:49,819
In fact, my wife loves backpacking.

154
00:08:50,060 --> 00:08:51,780
We always do a backpacking trip every year,

155
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:53,319
But I always curse at her.

156
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:55,460
I go. you know, this is the longest portage ever created.

157
00:08:55,740 --> 00:08:57,740
All you're doing is walking with a pack on.

158
00:08:57,880 --> 00:08:58,199
Yeah,

159
00:08:58,240 --> 00:08:59,960
There's no paddling involved at all.

160
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:00,560
Come on,

161
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:01,000
No,

162
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:01,680
What's with that?

163
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:04,599
There's a lot more canoeing in Ontario, isn't there?

164
00:09:05,219 --> 00:09:05,620
Oh, yeah,

165
00:09:05,719 --> 00:09:12,060
We've got so many bodies of water that really the canoe was invented for the landscape,

166
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:14,900
Or some say the landscape was invented for the canoe,

167
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:17,300
But it opened up

168
00:09:17,359 --> 00:09:21,000
Historically. it opened up Canada, especially Quebec and Ontario,

169
00:09:22,060 --> 00:09:28,000
And you know, Americans got, I guess, the chuck wagon and the cowboy hat, whatever got out of the west.

170
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,939
We had the voyager canoe.

171
00:09:29,940 --> 00:09:43,880
So for a little bit of driving, if you're coming from New York State, Ohio, Pennsylvania or any of the New England states, you can be in canoe paradise pretty quickly up over the line in Ontario.

172
00:09:44,440 --> 00:09:44,780
Well, yeah,

173
00:09:44,860 --> 00:09:53,680
If you're going from that area, you've got not only Algonquin, you've got Tomogamy, which is actually just an hour or maybe a couple hours north of Algonquin

174
00:09:53,680 --> 00:09:57,380
And it's a vast wilderness in endless rivers.

175
00:09:57,500 --> 00:10:07,239
The Sturgeon River is one of my favorite, which is, oh you know, for three days it's almost continual class three or class one, class two rapids

176
00:10:08,099 --> 00:10:09,459
And you won't see anybody

177
00:10:10,459 --> 00:10:14,780
There's. you were talking a little bit about the Upper Ottawa.

178
00:10:14,780 --> 00:10:18,280
Yeah, the Upper Ottawa, it was a kind of a miracle.

179
00:10:18,540 --> 00:10:22,699
My wife- actually my wife- has strict rules when we go on our holiday canoe trips.

180
00:10:22,959 --> 00:10:25,400
I'm not allowed to work and write about them.

181
00:10:27,180 --> 00:10:33,819
My daughter was turning six years old and she said: I want to go on another canoe trip for seven days.

182
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:38,100
I want to go somewhere we haven't gone before, which is not that easy because I go everywhere

183
00:10:38,100 --> 00:10:41,880
And I wanted to be an easy trip and not a lot of portaging.

184
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:46,280
Well, the Ottawa River. I never thought it would be the way it is,

185
00:10:46,380 --> 00:10:54,960
But up from Mattawa, which is where Samuel de Champagne Park is, we started there and we paddled down to what's called Driftwood Provincial Park,

186
00:10:55,120 --> 00:10:59,280
which is halfway between Tomogamy and the city of Ottawa,

187
00:10:59,980 --> 00:11:02,260
And you could do it in five days, but we did it in seven.

188
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:05,180
And there's no rapids, it's just a strong current.

189
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:08,979
There was one town halfway through and we got supplies there,

190
00:11:09,339 --> 00:11:12,920
But we saw 12 motorboats and that's it in seven days.

191
00:11:13,339 --> 00:11:15,300
And we saw one other canoe group

192
00:11:15,739 --> 00:11:16,819
And this is the Ottawa River,

193
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,140
Like everybody in Canada knows, the Ottawa River.

194
00:11:19,339 --> 00:11:23,479
So on one side you had the Laurentian Mountains and the other side you had the Ontario Bush,

195
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:27,120
And it was one of those things where you think you've seen it all.

196
00:11:27,339 --> 00:11:31,459
And then you realize that a lot of people ask me what's my favorite canoe trip,

197
00:11:31,460 --> 00:11:35,580
And it's always. it sounds bizarre, but my answer is the next one. I'm going on

198
00:11:35,720 --> 00:11:37,940
Because it really doesn't matter where you're going,

199
00:11:38,060 --> 00:11:39,360
It matters that you go. right,

200
00:11:39,820 --> 00:11:41,500
I think you're absolutely right.

201
00:11:42,080 --> 00:11:43,240
You just have to go.

202
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:52,040
There's just some marvelous opportunities for people to take a group of scouts up there and not to spend a whole lot of money,

203
00:11:52,820 --> 00:11:54,600
And you can have a marvelous trip.

204
00:11:54,980 --> 00:12:00,200
Just, I guess you could probably throw a dart at a map of Ontario and just go just about anywhere, couldn't you?

205
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:01,060
Oh yeah,

206
00:12:01,180 --> 00:12:06,580
And that's the nice thing about it too is that there are so many other places outside of parks you can paddle,

207
00:12:07,960 --> 00:12:12,820
Even in northwestern Ontario. a couple of years ago we went down what's called the Kopka River

208
00:12:13,300 --> 00:12:14,780
And it's south of Wabakimi.

209
00:12:14,940 --> 00:12:20,060
Now you can get to Wabakimi just by train from Toronto- the Toronto train station, for heaven's sakes,

210
00:12:20,140 --> 00:12:20,960
But they drop you off.

211
00:12:21,480 --> 00:12:22,760
But we went down the Kopka

212
00:12:23,080 --> 00:12:25,660
And it was one of those rivers that not many people knew.

213
00:12:25,800 --> 00:12:29,240
In fact it was Cliff Jacobson, an American, that she told me about it

214
00:12:29,240 --> 00:12:34,820
And we went down that river and we paddled to Nipigon, the north shore of Nipigon, which is an oasis,

215
00:12:35,040 --> 00:12:35,940
It's just so beautiful

216
00:12:36,320 --> 00:12:39,340
And it's one of the inland sort of Great Lakes.

217
00:12:40,020 --> 00:12:42,500
And we spent five weeks paddling

218
00:12:42,980 --> 00:12:44,420
And we weren't in the far north,

219
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:45,500
We didn't spend a fortune.

220
00:12:45,640 --> 00:12:47,980
In fact it didn't cost as much at all to do that river.

221
00:12:48,480 --> 00:12:50,380
So there's lots of places to go

222
00:12:50,460 --> 00:12:51,580
And it's pretty cheap.

223
00:12:51,580 --> 00:12:57,500
And any place that there's good paddling, usually there's going to be an outfitter nearby.

224
00:12:58,380 --> 00:13:00,340
Yeah, there usually is,

225
00:13:00,600 --> 00:13:08,000
And there's a lot of information nowadays on the computer, obviously, to where to get the information and if it's a good outfitter and that sort of thing,

226
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:18,480
But most likely in areas like really well-used areas like Nipigon, Tamagami, even Wabakimi or Quiddicko, there's at least a half a dozen outfitters that are going to help you out.

227
00:13:18,700 --> 00:13:20,820
You know, I mean I don't own a canoe.

228
00:13:22,160 --> 00:13:27,340
I do have a lovely paddle, which is kind of like having a leash without a puppy,

229
00:13:27,600 --> 00:13:31,300
But sometimes I think a paddle is more important to you than the canoe.

230
00:13:31,820 --> 00:13:32,620
You think about it.

231
00:13:32,620 --> 00:13:35,140
You paddle 10,000 strokes a day on a good paddle day.

232
00:13:35,920 --> 00:13:38,980
So, yeah, I'd sleep with my paddle if I were you.

233
00:13:41,140 --> 00:13:47,660
It is really pretty simple to get in with an outfitter and to find your way up there.

234
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:56,200
You know you're working on a book right now about some of the places to go canoeing in Ontario.

235
00:13:56,200 --> 00:14:03,340
Yeah, what I did is I wrote eight guidebooks throughout my years on where to go paddling in Quebec and Ontario

236
00:14:03,580 --> 00:14:08,620
And then my publisher said: can you do the best of like the Beatles White Album or whatever it's called,

237
00:14:08,680 --> 00:14:09,000
I don't know.

238
00:14:09,160 --> 00:14:11,780
But I said, yeah, I can do that,

239
00:14:11,840 --> 00:14:13,460
But I'd like to add new ones as well.

240
00:14:13,780 --> 00:14:19,240
And what they did is that I chose 40 of my favorite routes in Ontario and then 10 new ones

241
00:14:19,400 --> 00:14:20,460
And that will be out in March

242
00:14:20,600 --> 00:14:22,160
And it's called Ontario's Best Canoe Roots,

243
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:27,560
But there's some new ones though that really might interest you because it's not really being written about before.

244
00:14:27,780 --> 00:14:30,719
And there's an old canoe route in the south end of Tomagamy.

245
00:14:31,240 --> 00:14:32,839
There's a place called Chinacoochee.

246
00:14:33,219 --> 00:14:35,620
Now there's a place near Chinacoochee-

247
00:14:35,740 --> 00:14:39,620
This is just north of Ogonkin Park- that is called Killarney Park.

248
00:14:39,780 --> 00:14:42,860
Now that is the most scenic place in Ontario, Killarney Park.

249
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:44,319
It's got quartzite mountains,

250
00:14:44,900 --> 00:14:46,180
It's got turquoise water.

251
00:14:46,660 --> 00:14:49,920
But there's a place near there, near Sudbury, called Chinacoochee,

252
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:52,500
And it's a south access to Tomagamy.

253
00:14:52,939 --> 00:14:54,920
But what I did is I went up to Chinacoochee.

254
00:14:55,620 --> 00:14:58,219
Now they created that into a new park back in 1999..

255
00:14:58,540 --> 00:14:59,839
But they haven't done much to it,

256
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:01,579
So there's no reservation period.

257
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:03,500
Basically nobody really goes there.

258
00:15:04,180 --> 00:15:06,760
And what I did is I went up through Chinacoochee River

259
00:15:07,260 --> 00:15:09,120
But then I hooked up into Tomagamy.

260
00:15:09,240 --> 00:15:10,640
So I went through a bunch of Tomagamy lakes

261
00:15:10,979 --> 00:15:12,819
And then I hooked up to the Sturgeon River

262
00:15:12,880 --> 00:15:15,060
And I went down the Sturgeon River back to Chinacoochee.

263
00:15:15,060 --> 00:15:17,699
And it's six, maybe eight days,

264
00:15:18,079 --> 00:15:20,760
But it's one of the best canoe trips I can ever imagine,

265
00:15:20,859 --> 00:15:22,180
Because you've got a little bit about everything.

266
00:15:22,300 --> 00:15:25,119
You've got amazing scenery like the Quartzite Mountains,

267
00:15:25,479 --> 00:15:27,619
You've got a river that actually is doable.

268
00:15:27,719 --> 00:15:28,680
It's not a dangerous river.

269
00:15:28,859 --> 00:15:30,119
It's a very doable river

270
00:15:30,219 --> 00:15:31,020
But it's a lot of fun.

271
00:15:31,560 --> 00:15:35,000
And then you've got the big, huge white pine and Tomagamy you get to paddle through

272
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:36,959
And it's fantastic.

273
00:15:37,099 --> 00:15:38,579
And just make sure you go with a good person.

274
00:15:38,739 --> 00:15:40,540
Social dynamics is more important on a trip.

275
00:15:40,599 --> 00:15:41,839
You guys know that more than I do.

276
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:46,420
I suppose you could go on the trip you just described with somebody you didn't get along with very well

277
00:15:46,580 --> 00:15:50,280
And you wouldn't really be looking at the Quartzite Mountains all that much would you?

278
00:15:50,840 --> 00:15:51,720
Because here's the thing:

279
00:15:51,780 --> 00:15:53,780
When you're in the wilderness, you are who you are.

280
00:15:53,980 --> 00:15:55,780
There's no faking your character,

281
00:15:55,900 --> 00:15:56,640
There's no facade

282
00:15:57,220 --> 00:16:00,720
And if you don't like your boss, for example, the best thing to do is take them on a canoe trip in Maine,

283
00:16:01,720 --> 00:16:05,240
Because they can't fake themselves.

284
00:16:05,400 --> 00:16:06,120
And I love that.

285
00:16:06,180 --> 00:16:07,180
I love that about wilderness.

286
00:16:07,440 --> 00:16:08,820
I am who I am out there

287
00:16:08,820 --> 00:16:12,400
And you can't challenge nature or it will bite you back really bad.

288
00:16:12,520 --> 00:16:18,500
You have to sort of immerse yourself with it and become, in one sense, living out there.

289
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:28,920
So the people you're with now, even if you go canoe tripping with someone and you never meet them for 40 years, you finally meet them. you're still friends.

290
00:16:29,200 --> 00:16:29,380
Yeah,

291
00:16:29,860 --> 00:16:31,200
No, you're right about that,

292
00:16:31,320 --> 00:16:32,360
That's for sure

293
00:16:32,540 --> 00:16:38,020
Is that route that you were talking about one of those old routes that you've opened back up.

294
00:16:38,020 --> 00:16:38,500
Yeah,

295
00:16:38,900 --> 00:16:43,460
It's an old canoe route called a Nostogwin route, an old native route.

296
00:16:43,640 --> 00:16:48,380
There's another one that I did this year up by Bisco Tasting, up by Mississauga River.

297
00:16:48,540 --> 00:16:52,460
It's sort of central, north-central Ontario, north of Lake Huron

298
00:16:53,060 --> 00:16:54,500
And a couple of places up there,

299
00:16:54,540 --> 00:16:56,020
But one was Grey Owl.

300
00:16:56,300 --> 00:17:00,060
He traveled that area a lot when he was Archie Blaney, when he was a ranger,

301
00:17:00,060 --> 00:17:04,859
And he talks a lot about the Mississauga River, which is a beautiful river to do.

302
00:17:05,280 --> 00:17:07,940
But then I'm thinking: well, he had to have gone other places

303
00:17:08,240 --> 00:17:10,040
And I went to talk to a lot of natives up there

304
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:17,540
And they said, well, there was an old canoe route that was south of Bisco Tasting, where he lived,

305
00:17:17,540 --> 00:17:21,180
And it links up to what's called the Spanish River.

306
00:17:21,680 --> 00:17:30,520
And so I did a 12-day solo canoe loop through there and retraced it and found an old cabin that was kind of neat that I was told that he stayed at,

307
00:17:30,639 --> 00:17:33,420
And sure enough, the little scribblings inside, Archie Blaney,

308
00:17:34,520 --> 00:17:35,580
And it was kind of neat.

309
00:17:35,680 --> 00:17:37,740
Now we had huge problems here this summer.

310
00:17:37,940 --> 00:17:39,780
I'm not sure if you guys had, but we had no water.

311
00:17:40,440 --> 00:17:41,560
There was no water at all.

312
00:17:41,560 --> 00:17:53,720
And the weirdest thing is, because of that, I had to change my route all the time. places I thought I could go and I couldn't, which was kind of neat because I found other places that I would never have found.

313
00:17:53,820 --> 00:17:59,960
And I found this old lodge that was built in 1939 for the prince that was supposed to arrive

314
00:18:00,220 --> 00:18:01,440
And the locals had built it.

315
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:02,100
Oh yeah,

316
00:18:02,139 --> 00:18:04,080
I read, you did an article about that, didn't you?

317
00:18:04,240 --> 00:18:04,820
Yeah, yeah,

318
00:18:04,820 --> 00:18:09,080
And the neat thing about it is that there's a story about it, whether it's true or not,

319
00:18:09,080 --> 00:18:13,780
But I guess the locals say that he got off the train and the black flies were so bad, he went back on the train

320
00:18:14,540 --> 00:18:18,419
And they were so mad that he did that. they burned the lodge underground.

321
00:18:18,639 --> 00:18:20,240
There's nothing left of it except an old chimney,

322
00:18:20,320 --> 00:18:21,360
And I actually found that chimney.

323
00:18:21,500 --> 00:18:22,280
So that was kind of neat.

324
00:18:22,740 --> 00:18:24,460
I just I read that article.

325
00:18:24,600 --> 00:18:25,460
You reminded me of it

326
00:18:25,520 --> 00:18:26,580
And it was a cool article.

327
00:18:26,580 --> 00:18:34,980
And then, yeah, it gets to the denouement where this royal gets off the train and is besieged by black flies.

328
00:18:34,980 --> 00:18:42,960
And I thought there is. if somebody doesn't know Canada, there's like a perfect Canada story right there.

329
00:18:43,260 --> 00:18:43,760
Oh yeah,

330
00:18:43,980 --> 00:18:47,200
You know because you got there and oh yeah,

331
00:18:47,200 --> 00:18:51,960
And I've experienced the first year. we went up. we went up in June

332
00:18:52,680 --> 00:19:06,940
And we learned that going in August was a lot smarter because most of the black flies and the big giant biting flies and the mosquito airplanes were pretty much all gone in August.

333
00:19:06,940 --> 00:19:08,240
Yeah, it's true.

334
00:19:08,320 --> 00:19:13,600
So if I was going to take any time off, August, September is the best time to go.

335
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,460
I go out in June a lot, which is just insane,

336
00:19:16,580 --> 00:19:19,500
But I go out in June because I usually have lots of time in June

337
00:19:19,580 --> 00:19:22,100
But also nobody in the right mind would be able to.

338
00:19:24,240 --> 00:19:28,300
I mean, these things can like carry a person away.

339
00:19:28,500 --> 00:19:29,340
They're amazing.

340
00:19:30,340 --> 00:19:33,680
You think of what the black fly does to you.

341
00:19:33,680 --> 00:19:40,040
So if you try to symbolize what northern wilderness is in the springtime- a black fly- you don't feel it by you.

342
00:19:40,120 --> 00:19:43,240
It slices your skin so it doesn't really hurt at first,

343
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:45,860
And then it pulls your skin apart with stylets

344
00:19:46,060 --> 00:19:49,580
And then it slices you again and keeps slicing until it gets to the capillaries

345
00:19:49,960 --> 00:19:51,580
And then it starts lapping the blood up.

346
00:19:51,660 --> 00:19:56,860
And it needs to lap blood up for at least three minutes to get enough blood for protein for it to have its eggs.

347
00:19:57,320 --> 00:19:59,260
And then, when it's done it pulls its head out,

348
00:19:59,260 --> 00:20:03,220
But during that time it spits saliva in you so your blood doesn't clog late,

349
00:20:03,720 --> 00:20:03,760
Right?

350
00:20:03,920 --> 00:20:10,220
So if you actually hit the black fly while it's hitting you or biting you, you'll push all the saliva in you

351
00:20:10,280 --> 00:20:11,640
And that's what irritates the wound.

352
00:20:11,980 --> 00:20:16,360
And you think, if a million of those are doing that to you at one time, that's northern Ontario.

353
00:20:16,780 --> 00:20:17,040
Yeah,

354
00:20:17,940 --> 00:20:20,140
You know way too much about that, Kevin,

355
00:20:20,400 --> 00:20:21,280
Way too much.

356
00:20:21,780 --> 00:20:24,660
I'm going to be happy in my ignorance and come up in August.

357
00:20:25,220 --> 00:20:25,760
That's true.

358
00:20:25,760 --> 00:20:26,200
Yeah,

359
00:20:27,960 --> 00:20:31,660
So you do a fair amount of talking to scout groups.

360
00:20:31,660 --> 00:20:32,700
Yeah,

361
00:20:32,860 --> 00:20:33,800
I've always have.

362
00:20:33,980 --> 00:20:35,020
I mean, I was a scout

363
00:20:35,420 --> 00:20:39,420
And I also have a lot of background in outdoor education.

364
00:20:39,800 --> 00:20:41,420
Back in the 80s that's what I did

365
00:20:42,360 --> 00:20:44,500
And it's almost like payback time.

366
00:20:44,660 --> 00:20:48,960
And throughout the years I've always sort of volunteered to go to scout groups and give a talk,

367
00:20:49,020 --> 00:20:50,160
And it's usually a humorous talk.

368
00:20:50,220 --> 00:20:52,840
It's usually like sort of a burst of enthusiasm.

369
00:20:52,840 --> 00:20:54,620
This is why you guys should go out

370
00:20:54,679 --> 00:20:57,899
And this is why you did go out and that sort of thing.

371
00:20:58,540 --> 00:21:00,060
Tell me a little bit more about Grey Owl.

372
00:21:00,179 --> 00:21:06,000
I don't know that. a lot of people know exactly who that was and what the story is there.

373
00:21:06,340 --> 00:21:13,520
Well, I think a lot of scouts, scout leaders, would actually look into this character and just find it very intriguing for many reasons.

374
00:21:13,520 --> 00:21:19,360
He was an Englishman that really didn't like where he lived, didn't like his home, didn't like his family-

375
00:21:20,480 --> 00:21:21,920
I'm not sure if he didn't like his family-

376
00:21:22,060 --> 00:21:27,540
He just he was a dreamer and he wanted to become a woodsman and become a native in Canada.

377
00:21:27,840 --> 00:21:29,320
So he moved over to Canada-

378
00:21:29,440 --> 00:21:31,620
And this is back in the gosh 30s, 40s,

379
00:21:32,400 --> 00:21:35,780
And so what he did is he disguised himself up as a native

380
00:21:35,780 --> 00:21:41,180
And the natives knew. the natives knew that he wasn't a true native, but they just ignored him

381
00:21:41,240 --> 00:21:43,080
And they said: well, if he wants to be a native, he can be a native.

382
00:21:43,440 --> 00:21:46,879
But then he became a writer and then he became a conservationist,

383
00:21:46,960 --> 00:21:50,980
And he was one conservationist that saved the beaver from extinction. actually,

384
00:21:51,680 --> 00:21:53,240
He used to have a job trapping,

385
00:21:53,540 --> 00:21:55,680
And then one day he decided not to trap

386
00:21:55,860 --> 00:21:56,960
And then he wrote books about it

387
00:21:56,960 --> 00:22:06,580
And then he did films on his pet beavers that lived inside his cabin down in. you know, he was in Alberta in a cabin

388
00:22:07,100 --> 00:22:08,360
And he became a legend.

389
00:22:08,740 --> 00:22:15,380
But the thing is, the day after he died then the newspaper reports came out and said: oh, by the way, he wasn't a native,

390
00:22:15,640 --> 00:22:16,440
He was Archie Blaine.

391
00:22:17,280 --> 00:22:24,360
And you would think at that time, because of that, people would say, OK, well now the Wizard of Oz is now showing itself that sort of thing.

392
00:22:24,460 --> 00:22:25,460
How dare he do that?

393
00:22:25,720 --> 00:22:26,400
And he shunned,

394
00:22:26,400 --> 00:22:27,960
But he was still celebrated.

395
00:22:28,100 --> 00:22:29,420
He's still celebrated to this day.

396
00:22:29,840 --> 00:22:32,680
People are fine with the idea that he disguised.

397
00:22:32,680 --> 00:22:37,700
Well, now I know who to thank every time I have to pull a canoe over a beaver dam in Algona.

398
00:22:38,040 --> 00:22:38,820
Yeah, there you go,

399
00:22:39,280 --> 00:22:40,600
I'll be thinking about him.

400
00:22:41,040 --> 00:22:43,040
I got to tell you that's a good story.

401
00:22:43,280 --> 00:22:44,900
I paddled in the United States once,

402
00:22:45,140 --> 00:22:46,080
Oh, no more than once,

403
00:22:46,180 --> 00:22:50,180
But I do remember going to Maine in the Outer Randax

404
00:22:50,440 --> 00:22:52,480
And it's a busy place,

405
00:22:53,300 --> 00:22:55,020
And I was lining up to go paddling.

406
00:22:55,020 --> 00:22:57,080
But I went to this woman ahead of me

407
00:22:57,160 --> 00:22:59,460
And I said: well, so what's so special about this river?

408
00:22:59,540 --> 00:23:01,780
Everybody seems to be lining up to do this route.

409
00:23:01,879 --> 00:23:05,760
And she said: well, this is really good because it's got those things they have in Canada.

410
00:23:06,100 --> 00:23:09,120
What do they call their like? they make mounds of sticks.

411
00:23:09,260 --> 00:23:09,780
I went: beavers,

412
00:23:10,160 --> 00:23:10,520
Yeah, yeah,

413
00:23:10,520 --> 00:23:12,760
There's some beaver dams on this river.

414
00:23:12,879 --> 00:23:14,200
I went- you're kidding me?

415
00:23:14,480 --> 00:23:16,940
And I packed up my stuff and went back to Algona.

416
00:23:17,260 --> 00:23:17,379
It's like

417
00:23:18,080 --> 00:23:21,100
You mentioned bears.

418
00:23:21,480 --> 00:23:27,500
I think that there are some people below the parallel who think Canada is just crawling with them

419
00:23:27,620 --> 00:23:29,800
And it's a clear and present danger.

420
00:23:30,160 --> 00:23:34,240
What can you tell us about bears up in Ontario and environs?

421
00:23:34,240 --> 00:23:35,660
Well, we have the black bear here.

422
00:23:35,740 --> 00:23:36,540
We don't have the grizzly.

423
00:23:36,800 --> 00:23:39,660
It's not really an issue if you look at it statistically.

424
00:23:40,080 --> 00:23:46,460
Since 1936, there's only been seven deaths from a predacious black bear in Ontario-

425
00:23:46,520 --> 00:23:48,160
Actually, I think even Canada. actually,

426
00:23:48,660 --> 00:23:55,340
There's so much more of a chance of you getting hit on the highway by a car than a black bear attacking you.

427
00:23:55,760 --> 00:23:58,280
The only problem is I'm very bear phobic

428
00:23:58,280 --> 00:24:01,500
So I'm not going to be going and telling you that actually you know it's safe.

429
00:24:01,580 --> 00:24:02,300
You're okay out there,

430
00:24:02,360 --> 00:24:02,940
Don't worry about it,

431
00:24:02,940 --> 00:24:06,580
Because I'm still just so bear phobic out there as well.

432
00:24:07,040 --> 00:24:09,879
But I like the idea that bears scare people out there.

433
00:24:10,020 --> 00:24:12,600
It keeps a little bit more willingness out there, just like the black flies,

434
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:14,200
It keeps people on their toes.

435
00:24:14,660 --> 00:24:17,120
If you see a bear, it's a nice experience.

436
00:24:17,460 --> 00:24:19,280
It's a nice wildlife sighting

437
00:24:20,460 --> 00:24:24,420
And usually the bear as soon as it sees you will be gone in a second.

438
00:24:24,420 --> 00:24:32,840
If you spent your life not going into the wilderness because you're afraid of bears and then you got hit on the highway. it's not worth it.

439
00:24:32,900 --> 00:24:34,760
I'd rather actually spend my life in the wilderness

440
00:24:35,080 --> 00:24:40,720
And maybe one day, if it ever happens, that I get killed by a bear in the woods, well, it's better than getting killed on the highway,

441
00:24:41,140 --> 00:24:46,260
And it's so much easier for the survivors because you know there's not much to deal with once you're gone.

442
00:24:46,840 --> 00:24:47,780
No, that's true.

443
00:24:48,520 --> 00:24:50,160
I mean it's quite funny too.

444
00:24:50,160 --> 00:24:59,060
My wife always tells me to never tell these stories. but predacious bears, how they kill you is they take your head off because they think you're a loose guy, right.

445
00:24:59,620 --> 00:25:04,280
And so the studies show that even the people that got killed by these bears really didn't feel too much

446
00:25:05,920 --> 00:25:10,980
Okay. so I'm sure we've made everybody very comfortable about going into the woods up there.

447
00:25:10,980 --> 00:25:15,080
When I was in Algonquin at one point I was talking to one of the rangers there.

448
00:25:15,560 --> 00:25:19,880
Some of those guys go for several years between bear sightings and they're in the park every day.

449
00:25:20,880 --> 00:25:21,820
Yeah, it's funny.

450
00:25:22,980 --> 00:25:23,780
I see bears a lot.

451
00:25:23,940 --> 00:25:27,400
A Cree elder once told me that the bear was my spirit and I said: can I change that?

452
00:25:27,460 --> 00:25:28,560
Is there any way I can do that?

453
00:25:29,760 --> 00:25:32,700
But I was wondering why the bears always came into my camp.

454
00:25:32,760 --> 00:25:36,360
And he said: well, the bears know you're afraid of them and that's why they're harassing you more.

455
00:25:36,360 --> 00:25:42,860
And he taught me a lot. but the bear story I had this summer in Tomogamy, it's a classic, a family adventure.

456
00:25:43,139 --> 00:25:44,899
But the last morning we woke up

457
00:25:44,979 --> 00:25:50,080
We were on an island and thankfully I woke up early and I see this bear swimming to the island.

458
00:25:50,219 --> 00:25:56,379
And what that meant to me was this bear had got food on that island before, so it's coming to this island for food from our stuff.

459
00:25:56,860 --> 00:26:01,899
And I'm waking everybody up and say, okay, we're going to have to deal with a bear encounter here, so everybody get prepared.

460
00:26:02,520 --> 00:26:06,280
And my buddy was there and he goes: well, my kids haven't seen a bear yet.

461
00:26:06,360 --> 00:26:08,500
So could you wait before you scare it off?

462
00:26:08,540 --> 00:26:09,840
Could you wait for a while?

463
00:26:09,919 --> 00:26:11,399
I went- you're kidding me, right?

464
00:26:11,820 --> 00:26:13,020
I go, I want to scare this bear.

465
00:26:13,100 --> 00:26:14,500
He goes: well, wait until it gets halfway.

466
00:26:14,639 --> 00:26:16,659
And I said: look, once it's halfway, that's it,

467
00:26:16,679 --> 00:26:18,580
I'm going to start shooting the bear, bangers and stuff.

468
00:26:18,959 --> 00:26:20,100
Okay, okay, that's an agreement.

469
00:26:20,199 --> 00:26:23,120
So we get all the kids and they're watching the bear swim towards us and everybody's excited.

470
00:26:23,520 --> 00:26:25,760
And it happened to be the morning of my daughter's birthday

471
00:26:26,219 --> 00:26:27,800
And so she's all excited.

472
00:26:28,020 --> 00:26:32,199
And then so the bear's got halfway and I said, okay, I'm doing the call.

473
00:26:32,439 --> 00:26:34,580
So I get the bear banger, shoot it over nothing.

474
00:26:34,740 --> 00:26:36,060
The bear just keeps swimming towards us.

475
00:26:36,060 --> 00:26:37,340
I went, oh, this is not good.

476
00:26:37,679 --> 00:26:40,700
And then I shoot the flare and the bear just looks at me.

477
00:26:41,040 --> 00:26:44,399
Well then I ended up getting my paddle and started bashing the paddle against the rock,

478
00:26:44,440 --> 00:26:45,020
And that worked.

479
00:26:45,159 --> 00:26:46,860
The noise of that scared the bear off,

480
00:26:47,080 --> 00:26:51,520
But I broke the paddle and the guy that was with he made me that paddle

481
00:26:51,960 --> 00:26:53,840
And he looks at me. he goes: why did you do that?

482
00:26:53,860 --> 00:26:55,280
I go, I was saving your life

483
00:26:55,620 --> 00:27:00,659
And my daughter, right on cue, goes: dad, it was my birthday and that bear was coming to my party.

484
00:27:00,760 --> 00:27:02,060
How dare you scare him away?

485
00:27:02,060 --> 00:27:02,360
Yeah,

486
00:27:03,440 --> 00:27:06,639
So honestly, I was the only one afraid of this bear,

487
00:27:07,040 --> 00:27:09,240
So I thought it saved their lives.

488
00:27:12,679 --> 00:27:14,280
Your book's A Happy Camper.

489
00:27:14,580 --> 00:27:15,600
Tell me a little bit about it.

490
00:27:15,679 --> 00:27:17,679
That was an enjoyable book.

491
00:27:17,680 --> 00:27:21,280
Years ago I wrote a book called The Ways of the Wild-

492
00:27:21,400 --> 00:27:22,020
I think it was called,

493
00:27:22,100 --> 00:27:22,620
I'm not sure of it-

494
00:27:22,660 --> 00:27:25,180
And then I wanted to redo it into a how-to book

495
00:27:25,560 --> 00:27:27,940
And I ended up doing a little bit more sense of humor to it.

496
00:27:27,980 --> 00:27:30,720
I think it sold like 10,000 copies in the first month or something-

497
00:27:31,600 --> 00:27:34,100
Which in Canada, by the way, is huge,

498
00:27:34,460 --> 00:27:36,900
Because the best seller in Canada is 5,000 copies-

499
00:27:36,900 --> 00:27:41,300
And I was a little nervous about it because it's how-to,

500
00:27:41,460 --> 00:27:42,840
So I'm telling people what to do,

501
00:27:43,020 --> 00:27:45,540
But everybody says I'm a professional, but I'm not.

502
00:27:45,660 --> 00:27:49,600
I call myself a person that just loves camping, loves going out the outdoors,

503
00:27:50,120 --> 00:27:51,440
And I learn from experiences.

504
00:27:52,020 --> 00:27:54,120
So the book was based on my experiences.

505
00:27:54,760 --> 00:27:58,140
So it was basically: this is what not to do. because I did it

506
00:27:58,880 --> 00:28:01,680
And then after that they wanted me to do A Happy Camper 2.

507
00:28:01,780 --> 00:28:03,880
And I kind of didn't want to do A Happy Camper 2..

508
00:28:03,880 --> 00:28:05,920
So I did another book called Will and His Pleasures,

509
00:28:06,340 --> 00:28:10,100
And it was more about why we go and why we should keep going,

510
00:28:10,360 --> 00:28:12,120
And I really enjoyed writing that book.

511
00:28:13,520 --> 00:28:20,460
That sort of changed into a thing I'm doing last year called A Happy Camper Online Show

512
00:28:21,080 --> 00:28:23,740
And it's at kevincallumcom

513
00:28:23,820 --> 00:28:26,320
And it's basically just a bunch of videos, how-to videos,

514
00:28:26,460 --> 00:28:27,600
But it's all done with humor

515
00:28:27,980 --> 00:28:31,060
And just yesterday we launched the newest one.

516
00:28:31,200 --> 00:28:33,200
It was all how to choose a good camp hack

517
00:28:33,200 --> 00:28:34,960
And it's just silliness.

518
00:28:35,080 --> 00:28:36,340
We have a green screen behind me

519
00:28:36,500 --> 00:28:39,300
And the weather's changing every time I change my hat,

520
00:28:39,660 --> 00:28:41,280
But it's a lot of fun.

521
00:28:41,380 --> 00:28:43,740
Now Scouts- actually, they just contacted us

522
00:28:43,860 --> 00:28:50,060
And we just gave them the okay to put all the videos they want on their own website,

523
00:28:50,380 --> 00:28:51,480
And they are really great.

524
00:28:51,960 --> 00:28:55,700
I've put a couple of them on the Scoutmaster blog

525
00:28:56,180 --> 00:28:58,720
And I've gotten really favorable comments about them,

526
00:28:58,820 --> 00:29:00,800
But they're hilarious.

527
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:02,680
I really do enjoy those.

528
00:29:02,680 --> 00:29:07,720
And you've got four Water Walker Film Awards to your credit.

529
00:29:07,820 --> 00:29:10,400
Yeah, Water Walker is a thing they have in Canada.

530
00:29:10,720 --> 00:29:12,240
Well, it's all across the world actually,

531
00:29:12,360 --> 00:29:13,780
But it's based in Ottawa

532
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:19,240
And it's people from all over put their canoe films in for awards

533
00:29:19,440 --> 00:29:20,620
And I've won a few of them.

534
00:29:20,780 --> 00:29:22,080
There's so many places in the bush,

535
00:29:22,200 --> 00:29:23,740
You know. there's so many stories up there.

536
00:29:24,040 --> 00:29:25,520
How did we find the film?

537
00:29:25,620 --> 00:29:27,300
A YouTube site called Callen Canoe,

538
00:29:27,400 --> 00:29:27,980
Callen Canoe.

539
00:29:27,980 --> 00:29:30,440
If you go to Callen Canoe, there's like endless videos.

540
00:29:31,080 --> 00:29:33,300
I always take a camera with me when I go on trips

541
00:29:33,480 --> 00:29:34,680
And I do a lot of solo trips

542
00:29:34,760 --> 00:29:36,000
And I just have the camera and the canoe.

543
00:29:36,260 --> 00:29:41,140
Yeah, the Bisco Tasting route I did this year is all online on that site too.

544
00:29:41,140 --> 00:29:48,760
If I'm headed up to Ontario and I want to get like a taste of some of the canoe routes and some of the things up there. is there a good online resource for that?

545
00:29:49,220 --> 00:29:52,320
It's explore-mag M-A-G

546
00:29:52,840 --> 00:29:55,060
And it's Explore Magazine that I write for.

547
00:29:55,160 --> 00:29:56,440
But I have my blog on there

548
00:29:56,780 --> 00:30:00,340
And what I do a lot on that blog is I write a lot of my trip journal notes,

549
00:30:01,120 --> 00:30:03,320
So scouts would actually get a lot of that.

550
00:30:03,879 --> 00:30:10,920
And even one trip I did a couple years ago was up by Elliott Lake where we reopened a canoe trip that closed in the 1980s

551
00:30:11,640 --> 00:30:14,140
And rainbow trout were stocked there in 1942.

552
00:30:14,460 --> 00:30:15,640
And they self-sustained themselves

553
00:30:15,640 --> 00:30:20,380
And we got into these lakes where we're catching 10-pound rainbows that had never seen a fly.

554
00:30:20,980 --> 00:30:25,580
I'm hosting a contest that is called the Classic Canadian Adventure.

555
00:30:26,120 --> 00:30:34,340
But if you win- and anybody in North America can win- you and your three buddies go seven days with me

556
00:30:34,540 --> 00:30:35,660
And we go to Ottawa.

557
00:30:35,980 --> 00:30:37,920
We go whitewater rafting, whitewater kayaking,

558
00:30:38,180 --> 00:30:41,860
We go flying through Barron Canyon and the Gawkin Park with the M&R turbo plane.

559
00:30:41,860 --> 00:30:45,939
So, yeah, we go to Bonachere Park and all these other things.

560
00:30:46,080 --> 00:30:47,620
So it's kind of a neat contest

561
00:30:47,860 --> 00:30:50,620
And so some scouts might be interested in that.

562
00:30:51,100 --> 00:30:54,100
Let me make sure I understood exactly where do we find the contest.

563
00:30:54,300 --> 00:30:57,959
But if you just go to my blog, there's a write-up on it.

564
00:30:58,040 --> 00:30:58,479
It's a video.

565
00:30:58,979 --> 00:31:00,679
Hey, I'd love to do this again sometime.

566
00:31:01,040 --> 00:31:01,520
No problem,

567
00:31:01,520 --> 00:31:07,840
And actually we can. even if I'm in a Gawkin Park on a canoe trip, I can actually do the same thing.

568
00:31:07,920 --> 00:31:08,520
Oh, no, kidding.

569
00:31:09,060 --> 00:31:10,560
Yeah, it's called Began.

570
00:31:10,800 --> 00:31:13,300
It's a satellite system that a company let us borrow.

571
00:31:13,580 --> 00:31:13,860
Wow,

572
00:31:14,260 --> 00:31:20,080
I do a lot of TV stuff like that or radio stuff where I'm actually live in the park,

573
00:31:20,460 --> 00:31:22,840
And they even do it by way of video,

574
00:31:23,060 --> 00:31:24,580
So you can actually see me in the park.

575
00:31:24,620 --> 00:31:25,900
Are you going out at all in the winter?

576
00:31:26,620 --> 00:31:27,920
Yeah, in January, I am.

577
00:31:27,940 --> 00:31:30,340
I'm doing a winter trip in the end of January

578
00:31:30,540 --> 00:31:31,480
In Agonquin actually.

579
00:31:31,640 --> 00:31:33,300
Oh, that would be really cool.

580
00:31:33,460 --> 00:31:34,360
Yeah, it'd be fun.

581
00:31:34,740 --> 00:31:36,780
So, Kevin, thanks so much for joining us.

582
00:31:37,660 --> 00:31:39,620
Had a great time talking to you.

583
00:31:39,960 --> 00:31:43,900
Hope we interested some people into checking out canoeing up there in Ontario.

